---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const b = appBySlug('recart')!;
const path = '/compare/brevo-vs-recart';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-recart');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-recart');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Recart replace Brevo email?",
    "answer": "Recart presents managed SMS with email partner integrations. Confirm the email provider and the actual handoff before moving a Brevo flow."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must a Brevo import preserve?",
    "answer": "Check the linked account, subscription status and configured consent authority before historical import or resync. An imported contact is not automatically eligible for every channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "Who approves managed SMS?",
    "answer": "Name a merchant approver, the Recart operating contact and an emergency pause owner. Agree how purchase and unsubscribe state reaches each queue."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the example prove payback?",
    "answer": "No. The figures are hypothetical gross profit with fixed goods cost, before messaging, shipping, returns, fees and staff costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare a Shopify account import with a managed SMS handoff beside email.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label:'Evaluate Brevo',tool:a.name,reason:'Your team owns the linked account, permission reconciliation and email queue.'},{label:'Evaluate Recart',tool:b.name,reason:'You want managed SMS beside a confirmed email provider.'}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Recart</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Operating model</td><td>Confirm the Brevo PushOwl account and import configuration</td><td>Managed SMS with email partner handoff</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance check</td><td>Permission state survives import and resync</td><td>Merchant approvals and both queues see purchase exits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Quote actual account, automation and channel usage</td><td>Quote service scope, message usage and retained email provider</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Set consent authority before importing</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s Shopify connection guide</a> uses Brevo PushOwl and warns that historical import can overwrite contact attributes including subscription status. Confirm the destination account before import, especially when a direct Brevo account already exists. Installation, account linking and historical import need separate checks.</p>
<p>The current <a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14660088-source-of-truth-for-marketing-consent">PushOwl consent guide</a> makes the source of truth configurable. Choosing Shopify authority for a channel allows Shopify status to override Brevo; Brevo authority protects existing Brevo consent while new contacts can still arrive from Shopify. Record the selected authority and test unsubscribed, email-only and SMS-only profiles through import and resync before releasing reminders. Preserve opt-out evidence rather than inferring permission from a successful sync.</p>
<h2>Buy an SMS operating handoff, not an assumed email replacement</h2>
<p><a href="https://recart.com/fully-managed-sms">Recart’s managed SMS page</a> describes managed SMS and a network of email marketing partners. Establish which provider will send email and whether the required integration supports the state your flows need. Do not infer native email replacement, push delivery or shared suppression from a partner listing. Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<p>Ask for a release contract: who drafts and approves messages, changes audiences, checks offers, handles quiet-hour configuration and pauses a campaign when inventory changes? A managed service still needs a merchant who can approve a shipping explanation or product substitution. Compare that effort with the team required to maintain the Brevo email queue.</p>
<h2>Demonstrate the two queues with one checkout</h2>
<p>Proposed exercise: use a test customer with documented channel permissions and record the Shopify customer, checkout and order identifiers. Enter recovery email and SMS, purchase while a reminder waits, then inspect both queues. Repeat with an unsubscribe and a repeated event. Document which integration delivers each update and who reconciles a failed handoff. An email address match alone does not prove the correct cart or permission was selected.</p>
<p>Keep one owner for the recovery offer. A mobile signup may already have a welcome code; test its combination with a cart code before approval. Choose timing from your purchase cycle, support coverage and channel eligibility. No universal touch cap, SMS urgency rule or fifteen-minute purchase exit is established by this comparison.</p>
<h2>Resolve the reason for abandonment before offering a code</h2>
<p>For unavailable sizes, rebuild a purchasable basket before sending a link. For a gift-message or financing interruption, check that the return link preserves the relevant checkout context. A shipping quote or B2B minimum may need a support answer; a discount does not fix eligibility. Verify subscription cadence and bundle pricing in the recovered checkout, and distinguish a repeat buyer’s new order from the earlier purchase used for suppression.</p>
<h2>Model discount cost separately from migration cost</h2>
<p>Hypothetical example: a $345 basket at 58% gross margin has $200.10 gross profit at full price. With fixed goods cost, a 13% code removes $44.85, leaving $155.25. A second $377 basket at 54% margin has $203.58 at full price; an illustrative 18% code removes $67.86, leaving $135.72. These percentages are examples, not recommended caps. Both exclude messaging, shipping, tax, returns, payment fees and staff costs.</p>
<p>Add account reconciliation, template rebuilding, approval time and any retained email subscription to the migration budget. Measure incremental orders with a suitably designed comparison before claiming payback; platform-attributed revenue alone cannot establish that the service paid for itself.</p>
<h2>Cut over without replaying stale recovery</h2>
<p>Export permission and suppression records, map event identifiers and inventory fields, and rebuild the cart flow in a paused state. Compare intended entries without sending duplicate messages. Pause the old owner and inspect waiting sends before activating its replacement. Keep a rollback record identifying which queue can resume and which events were already handled. Do not replay historical carts merely because their customer records imported successfully.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
